# Bank System Project Rules

When the user asks to review code, take these rules into account (`учитывай`).

Keep review feedback short: 1–2 sentences, point out the issue, give a hint — do not rewrite their code unless they ask.

## Project goal

Build a multi-level banking system with account models, transactions, audit, reporting, security ideas, and extensibility — as OOP modules in Python.

The project has 7 stages (7 days) plus final integration. Each stage should deepen the previous one.

## Boundaries

- Keep data in memory only — no database.
- No external APIs or third-party integrations.
- Financial entities are educational OOP models, not a real banking or investment system.

## Overall concept

Support:

- different bank account types
- account operations with logging and audit
- centralized management of all bank clients
- transaction history with extended parameters
- analytical report generation
- data validation and an error system
- extensibility for future account types and operations

## Key principles to check in reviews

Prefer designs that show:

- encapsulation
- inheritance
- polymorphism
- abstraction
- SOLID principles
- domain modeling
- structured logging
- modules ready for unit testing

## Review focus

Judge the code against the current homework day first, then against these project principles — only mention principles that are relevant to what they asked you to review.
